I've sent my report to the caller. There is a candidate implementation, but it has only been tested against my own cases, never against GNU sed 4.9 itself. My 90 cases come mostly from the manual's examples. 89 pass, and the one failure was my own wrong expected output, not a bug.

The two files, both in `environment/app/pysed/`:
- **`sed.py`** (rewritten) handles options, parsing the script, addresses, all the required commands, and reading input.
- **`posixre.py`** (new) is a pure-Python regex engine for basic and extended syntax that finds the POSIX leftmost-longest match. It doesn't use Python's `re`.

A 60-line input joined into one line ran in under 0.1 seconds. The program starts no other processes and loads no native code. I removed the test files and `__pycache__` I had left in `pysed/`.

The main guesses about GNU's behaviour are all in the report:
- whether `D` flushes queued `a` text and resets the `t` flag when it restarts the cycle (I assumed it does neither);
- whether `n`/`N` reset the `t` flag (I assumed they do);
- how escapes inside `a`/`i`/`c` text are handled;
- whether labels for `b`/`t`/`T` stop at `}`;
- whether `q`'s exit code wins when an input file was missing;
- how a special delimiter character is treated when it is escaped inside the regex;
- always adding a final newline to output lines.
